relic NEWS -- changes a user or an embedder of relic would notice, by release

Unreleased

  * The website's performance guide includes browser-rendered benchmark
    charts for one and sixteen threads, accessible timing tables, and
    references for the algorithms and engineering used by relic.
  * The website includes a browser playground that evaluates programs locally
    with WebAssembly, examples, diagnostics and cancellable runs.
  * An optional Emscripten build produces a reusable JavaScript module for
    browsers and Node.js. Its asynchronous evaluate() interface preserves
    signed 64-bit integers and arbitrary string bytes, enforces resource
    limits, and returns owned results. Native builds require no new runtime
    dependency. Generated artifacts are built during website deployment.

relic 1.0.0 (2026-11-04)

  The first release: a Datalog engine for C, as a library, librelic, and a
  command-line tool, relic(1).

  Language

  * A program is facts, rules and queries.  Values are symbols, strings
    and 64-bit integers.  They are distinct types: the symbol foo and the
    string "foo" are never equal, and ordering values of different types
    with <, <=, > or >= is false.
  * Negation is stratified.  In a negated atom, _ is a column the atom
    does not test, so not order(_, C, _) holds when C has no order at all.
    A program that recurses through negation or an aggregate is rejected.
  * Comparisons accept integer expressions with + - * / mod, unary minus
    and parentheses.  An equality assigns a variable nothing else binds.
    A result outside 64 bits and a division by zero are errors, not
    wrapped values.
  * Six string functions work on bytes: length, cat, substr, index, match
    and str.  match takes a regular expression like those of grep -E, less
    counted repetition, character classes and back-references.  It runs in
    time linear in the string, so no pattern can make it backtrack.
  * The aggregates count, sum, min and max fold the rows of a body in
    braces into one value.
  * There are no floating-point numbers.  A number such as 1.5 or 1e5 is
    an error, because those forms are reserved.  So are # and $ outside a
    string or a comment.

  Evaluation

  * Recursive rules are evaluated to a fixed point, using indexes and a
    join order chosen from estimated cost.
  * A query that names constants is answered through a Magic Sets rewrite,
    so relic derives what the query can reach rather than every relation.
    A program the rewrite does not suit, or whose rewrite would grow too
    large, is evaluated in full instead, with a warning in the second
    case.  The answers never differ, but evaluating in full can meet an
    arithmetic or string error that the rewrite would have avoided.
  * Evaluation and the import of large tables can run on several threads.
    The default is one; -j chooses more.
    The answers, their order and the diagnostics are the same whatever the
    number of threads.  Small programs stay on one thread.
  * Without -s, the order of the rows is unspecified but the same on every
    run over the same input.
  * Optional limits cap the tuples held, the memory that relations,
    values, plans and rewrites occupy, and the size of one program text or
    one field of an imported table.  A load, an import, an evaluation or a
    query that would exceed a limit is refused with a diagnostic.  A failed
    load or import adds no facts or program statements; interned values and
    reserved capacity can remain after failure.  A finite memory limit can
    make some evaluations slower.
  * A query that fails delivers no rows rather than a truncated answer.
    Only an interrupted query can have delivered a prefix of its rows.

  The relic(1) tool

  * Answers are written as text, CSV, TSV or JSON, with -F.  -s sorts them.
    In text, and in CSV and TSV written to a terminal, control characters
    and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are escaped, so results are safe to
    print.  Written to a file or a pipe, CSV and TSV hold each value's
    bytes and read back unchanged.
  * Facts are imported from CSV and TSV tables with -I.  A file name such
    as edge.tsv gives the predicate and the format, and
    -T edge:symbol,symbol declares the column types once.  A table is
    added entirely or not at all.
  * Whole relations are written with -O, as CSV, TSV, JSON, or as facts
    with -O path.dl that a later run loads as a program.
  * -o and -O refuse to write a file that the run also reads or writes: a
    program file, an imported table, redirected standard input or output,
    or another output.  Files are compared by identity, so a link to a
    file is the file.
    The refusal is a usage error, reported before anything is read or
    written.
  * -q answers a query given on the command line, -c checks the program and
    its tables and evaluates nothing, -a evaluates the whole program
    before answering, -n writes no rows, and -v and -vv say more on the
    standard error.
  * -j threads shares evaluation among threads; the default is 1, and
    -j 0 uses one per processor, up to 256.  -l limit=value sets a
    resource limit.
  * -i starts an interactive session with line editing where libedit is
    present.  Control-C stops the running query and returns to the prompt.
  * -e writes the plan of every evaluation.  -X selects internal behaviours
    for testing and debugging, including -X stats for the evaluation
    counters.  The plan text and the -X switches are not stable.
  * The tool checks everything before it evaluates anything, and reports
    every mistake found.  It exits 1 for any failure, and 2 for a
    malformed command line.

  The C library

  * One header, <relic.h>, strict ISO C11 and usable from C++.  A
    pkg-config module, librelic, supplies the flags.  Only symbols that
    begin with relic_ are exported.
  * A program creates a context with relic_create() or
    relic_create_config(), loads text, files or single facts, imports
    tables, and runs queries.  A query's rows go to a callback as they are
    derived, or are collected into a result that holds its own copy of
    every symbol and string and outlives the context.
  * A configuration, an import and the statistics are passed with their
    size: the caller passes sizeof of the structure, and a later release
    can add fields at its end.  A program built against one release runs
    against an older or a newer one, as long as it leaves the fields the
    older one lacks at zero.
  * Every failure is reported by return value: -1 from a function that
    returns int, NULL from one that returns a pointer.  A call on a context
    explains the failure through relic_error(), relic_error_class() and the
    diagnostic callback.  No ordinary failure exits the process.
  * relic_error_class() says what kind of failure it was: input the
    language rejects, an evaluation error, a limit, a file, memory, an
    interrupt, a malformed call or a bug in relic.  A program need not
    read the text.
  * relic_interrupt() stops an evaluation in progress, from a signal
    handler, another thread or a callback, and leaves the context ready
    for the next.
  * Contexts share nothing.  One context is used by one thread at a time;
    relic_interrupt() is the one call another thread may make on it.  A
    collected result is immutable, and any number of threads may read it.
  * The RELIC_UNSTABLE_ configuration switches select alternative
    evaluation strategies for testing and comparison.  They are not part of
    the interface: a release may change or remove one, and a program must
    not depend on them.
  * relic(3) documents every function.

  Requirements and platforms

  * No third-party runtime dependencies.  A C11 compiler, make and a POSIX
    shell build a release tarball.  libedit is optional, for line editing
    in the interactive mode, and --disable-threads builds without threads.
    A git checkout also needs the autotools.
  * --enable-thinlto builds with LLVM link-time optimization, after
    configuration checks that the compiler, archiver and linker support it.
  * Built and tested on macOS, on Linux with glibc and with musl, and on
    FreeBSD.  OpenBSD and NetBSD are portability targets without release
    validation.
